  "analysis": "The problem is dynamic: after each morning's new friendship (edge), we need the size of the k-core of the current graph (the largest set where every included person has at least k friends within the set). A straightforward online recomputation after each edge would be too slow. A standard offline approach is to process edges in reverse. Build the final graph with all m edges, compute its k-core (remove vertices with degree < k iteratively). Then, process edges in reverse: before removing edge i, the current k-core size is the answer for day i. Remove edge i from the graph; if its endpoints are still alive, decrease their degrees and, if any falls below k, remove that vertex and propagate removals to its neighbors through non-removed edges. Each vertex is removed at most once and each edge considered a bounded number of times, giving O(n + m) time and memory, which fits the limits.",
  "plan": "1) Implement a reverse-edge k-core maintenance algorithm in Python.
- Build adjacency lists with edge indices for all m edges.
- Compute initial k-core on the full graph: deg[v] = number of incident edges; push vertices with deg[v] < k into a queue and remove them, updating neighbors' deg when edges are not removed.
- Maintain an alive[v] flag and an removed_edge[i] flag. current_alive_count tracks size of current k-core.
- For i from m down to 1: set ans[i] = current_alive_count; then remove edge i: mark removed_edge[i] = True; if both endpoints are alive, decrement their deg. If deg falls below k for any endpoint, push it into a queue and propagate removals: mark alive[v]=False, current_alive_count--, and for each incident edge not yet removed, decrement neighbor deg and enqueue neighbors whose deg drops below k.
- After finishing, output ans[1..m].
